\title{The Exact Algebra of Overlapping Measurement Legs\\[2mm]
\large Variance defects, lineage bounds, and cluster ceilings for estimators pooled from a shared random stream}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{25 August 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
Large computational experiments are routinely extended, re-run, and pooled. When the extensions are generated from the same pseudorandom master seed, the resulting ``replication legs'' are not statistically independent, and the standard inverse-variance combination silently understates the variance of the pooled estimator. We give a complete and exact account of this phenomenon in a Hilbert-space model of correlated measurements, in which the inner product of two centred readouts is their covariance.

Four results form the core. First, for sample means cut from a single stream of pairwise uncorrelated draws of common variance $\sigma^2$, the covariance of two legs is exactly $\sigma^2|S\cap T|/(|S||T|)$, whence the \emph{exact defect law}: the true variance of a weighted pool exceeds the independence bookkeeping by precisely $2w(1-w)\sigma^2|S\cap T|/(|S||T|)$, with equality to the naive value \textbf{if and only if} the legs are disjoint. Second, a \emph{lineage bound}: for any chain $S_1\subseteq\cdots\subseteq S_n$ of legs from one stream and any convex weighting, the pooled variance is at least $\sigma^2/|S_n|$, and more generally, for an arbitrary overlapping family, at least $\sigma^2/|\bigcup_l S_l|$ --- a bound depending on nothing but the number of distinct draws consumed, and attained by the uniform average over that union. Third, a \emph{population overlap identity} for two-level designs, $\Cov = \rho\sigma^2|K\cap K'|/(|K||K'|) + (1-\rho)\sigma^2|K\cap K'||T\cap T'|/(|K||T||K'||T'|)$, exhibiting shared-draw and shared-population dependence as the two summands of one formula. Fourth, a \emph{cluster ceiling}: the grand mean over $k$ clusters of $m$ equicorrelated draws has variance $\sigma^2(1+(m-1)\rho)/(km) \ge \rho\sigma^2/k$ uniformly in $m$, with the floor attained in the limit, so the effective sample size never exceeds $k/\rho$.

We complete the picture with the generalised-least-squares rule for two correlated legs: the optimal weight is $w^\star=(v_2-c)/(v_1+v_2-2c)$ with floor $(v_1v_2-c^2)/(v_1+v_2-2c)$; inverse-variance weighting is optimal exactly when $c(v_2-v_1)=0$; the nested optimum is $w^\star=0$; and disjoint legs restore the classical weights exactly. Applied to a randomness experiment of $128$ moduli $\times$ $600\,000$ paired samples ($76.8$ million pairs), the results retract a three-leg combination that treated a run and its own strict prefix as independent, and dissolve an apparent threshold crossing in a two-leg fallback whose legs share no draws but whose populations are nested: at the audited nesting ratio $|T|=4|S|$ the inflation is exactly $7/5$, so any reported two-sided statistic of at most $2.14$ has honest statistic below $1.96$. With $k=128$ and intra-modulus correlation $\rho=10^{-2}$, the run carries no more information than $12\,800$ independent evaluations.
\end{abstract}

\noindent\textbf{Keywords:} design effect, intra-cluster correlation, effective sample size, inverse-variance weighting, generalised least squares, shared random streams, replication, variance inflation.

\section{Introduction}

\subsection{The problem}

A computational experiment produces an estimate $\hat\theta_1$ with a standard error. It is re-run longer, producing $\hat\theta_2$ with a smaller one. The instinct is to combine them by precision: if the variances are $v_1,v_2$ and the estimates are independent, the inverse-variance weighted combination $\hat\theta = w\hat\theta_1 + (1-w)\hat\theta_2$ with $w = v_2/(v_1+v_2)$ has variance $v_1v_2/(v_1+v_2)$, smaller than either.

Independence is doing all the work, and in a deterministic pseudorandom pipeline it is frequently false in a way invisible from summary statistics. If the second run consumes the same seeded stream as the first, extended, then the second run's data \emph{contains} the first's: the legs are not merely correlated, the short one is a subset of the long one. Inverse-variance weighting then reports a variance that is too small by an amount we compute exactly, and intervals derived from it are too narrow by a factor we also compute exactly.

There is a subtler failure. Two legs may consume disjoint draws --- different code paths, no shared random consumption --- and remain dependent because they measure the \emph{same objects}. In a two-level design where objects carry idiosyncratic components, sharing objects induces covariance even under complete draw-disjointness. If the object population is itself generated from the master seed, ``re-drawing the population'' inside a fixed stream reproduces the same objects and hence the same dependence.

\subsection{Contributions}

We work coordinate-free: centred readouts are vectors in a real inner product space $E$, with $\inner{x}{y}$ read as covariance and $\inner{x}{x}$ as variance. Every statement below is then an algebraic identity in finitely many cardinalities, valid for any joint distribution with the stated second moments. We establish: the overlap master identity and exact defect law (\S3); quantification of the two audited failure modes, including the $7/5$ inflation and the resulting retraction (\S4); the lineage and distinct-draw bounds (\S5); the population overlap identity (\S6); the cluster ceiling (\S7); the generalised-least-squares repair (\S8); the applied audit (\S9) and the resulting design rule (\S10). All models are shown realisable (\S2.3), so no statement is vacuous.

\section{The model}

\subsection{Covariance as inner product}

Let $E$ be a real inner product space. We interpret $x\in E$ as a centred scalar readout, $\inner{x}{y}$ as $\Cov(x,y)$ and $\inner{x}{x}$ as $\Var(x)$. Bilinearity of the inner product is bilinearity of covariance.

\begin{definition}[Design]
A \emph{design} consists of vectors $(x_i)_{i\in\mathbb N}$ in $E$ (the \emph{draws}, indexed by stream position) and a scale $\sigma>0$ with $\inner{x_i}{x_i}=\sigma^2$ for all $i$ and $\inner{x_i}{x_j}=0$ for $i\ne j$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Leg]
For finite nonempty $S\subseteq\mathbb N$, the \emph{leg} indexed by $S$ is $\bar x_S := |S|^{-1}\sum_{i\in S}x_i$.
\end{definition}

A leg models one measurement run: $S$ is the set of stream positions that run consumed. Two runs from the same seeded stream overlap precisely on $S\cap T$; runs from independent seeds have $S\cap T=\varnothing$ after relabelling.

\subsection{The combinatorial core}

\begin{lemma}[Orthogonal partial sums]\label{lem:core}
Let $(f_p)_{p\in\iota}$ satisfy $\inner{f_p}{f_p}=v$ for all $p$ and $\inner{f_p}{f_q}=0$ for $p\ne q$. Then for finite $S,T\subseteq\iota$,
\[ \Bigl\langle \sum_{p\in S} f_p,\ \sum_{q\in T} f_q \Bigr\rangle = v\,|S\cap T| . \]
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Expand bilinearly. For fixed $p\in S$, orthogonality collapses $\inner{f_p}{\sum_{q\in T}f_q}$ to $v$ if $p\in T$ and to $0$ otherwise; summing the indicator over $S$ gives $v|S\cap T|$.
\end{proof}

Lemma~\ref{lem:core} is stated for an arbitrary index type deliberately: it is applied to stream positions, to clusters, and to (cluster, draw) pairs alike.

\subsection{Non-vacuity}

\begin{proposition}
For every $\sigma>0$ a design exists: take $E=\ell^2(\mathbb N)$ and $x_i=\sigma e_i$ with $(e_i)$ the standard orthonormal basis. The two-level models of \S6--\S7 are likewise realised in $\ell^2$ of a disjoint union, with cluster component of norm $\sqrt{\rho}\,\sigma$ and private component of norm $\sqrt{1-\rho}\,\sigma$, for every $0\le\rho\le1$.
\end{proposition}

\section{The overlap identity and the exact defect law}

\begin{theorem}[Master identity]\label{thm:master}
For any two legs of a design,
\[ \Cov(\bar x_S,\bar x_T) = \frac{\sigma^2\,|S\cap T|}{|S|\,|T|}. \]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Apply Lemma~\ref{lem:core} with $v=\sigma^2$ to the unnormalised sums and divide by $|S||T|$.
\end{proof}

Three consequences: $\Var(\bar x_S)=\sigma^2/|S|$; disjoint legs are uncorrelated; and if $S\subseteq T$ then $\Cov(\bar x_S,\bar x_T)=\sigma^2/|T|=\Var(\bar x_T)$ --- \emph{nesting is total correlation with the long leg}, so the prefix carries no information the superset lacks.

Fix $w\in\mathbb R$ and set $\hat\theta_w := w\bar x_S + (1-w)\bar x_T$.

\begin{definition}
The \emph{reported} variance is $\Vrep(w) := w^2\sigma^2/|S| + (1-w)^2\sigma^2/|T|$; the \emph{true} variance is $\Vtrue(w):=\Var(\hat\theta_w)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Exact defect law]\label{thm:defect}
\[ \Vtrue(w) = \Vrep(w) + \frac{2w(1-w)\,\sigma^2\,|S\cap T|}{|S|\,|T|}. \]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Expand $\inner{\hat\theta_w}{\hat\theta_w}$ into three terms and apply Theorem~\ref{thm:master} to each.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
For $0\le w\le 1$ we have $\Vrep(w)\le\Vtrue(w)$: independence bookkeeping never overstates the variance of a convex pool from a shared stream.
\end{corollary}

\begin{theorem}[Independence is disjointness]\label{thm:iff}
For $0<w<1$ and nonempty $S,T$: $\Vtrue(w)=\Vrep(w)$ if and only if $S\cap T=\varnothing$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The defect term is a product of strictly positive factors with $|S\cap T|$, and vanishes exactly when the intersection is empty.
\end{proof}

Theorem~\ref{thm:iff} is the conceptual heart: for sample means cut from one stream, statistical independence \emph{is} set-theoretic disjointness. There is no weaker sufficient condition, and no way to certify independence from summary statistics --- one must inspect which draws were consumed.

\section{The two audited failure modes}

\subsection{A dataset pooled with itself}

\begin{theorem}[Duplicate leg]\label{thm:dup}
For $S=T$ and $w=1/2$, $\Vtrue = \sigma^2/|S| = 2\Vrep$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$\hat\theta_{1/2}=\bar x_S$ has variance $\sigma^2/|S|$, while the reported value is $\tfrac14\sigma^2/|S|+\tfrac14\sigma^2/|S| = \sigma^2/(2|S|)$.
\end{proof}

Counting one dataset twice halves the reported variance and shrinks half-widths by exactly $\sqrt2$: a factor-of-two arithmetic error, not a modelling nuance.

\subsection{A prefix pooled with its superset}

Let $S\subseteq T$, $|S|<|T|$, and $\wivw := |S|/(|S|+|T|)$.

\begin{theorem}
$\Vrep(\wivw) = \sigma^2/(|S|+|T|)$, the ``pooled sample size'' value.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[True variance of a nested pool]\label{thm:nested}
\[ \Vtrue(\wivw) = \frac{\sigma^2\bigl(3|S|+|T|\bigr)}{\bigl(|S|+|T|\bigr)^2} = \frac{3|S|+|T|}{|S|+|T|}\,\Vrep(\wivw), \]
and the inflation factor $(3|S|+|T|)/(|S|+|T|)$ exceeds $1$ for all nonempty $S,T$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Theorem~\ref{thm:defect} with $|S\cap T|=|S|$, then simplify.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Pooling is worse than discarding]\label{thm:worse}
For $S\subseteq T$ with $|S|<|T|$: $\sigma^2/|T| < \Vtrue(\wivw)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Clearing denominators reduces the inequality to $(|T|-|S|)^2>0$.
\end{proof}

Thus ``pooling more legs cannot hurt'' is false for overlapping legs; the correct statement is that pooling more \emph{distinct draws} cannot hurt.

\begin{theorem}[Nested variance at arbitrary weight]\label{thm:nestw}
For $S\subseteq T$: $\Vtrue(w) = \sigma^2\bigl(w^2/|S| + (1-w^2)/|T|\bigr)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{corollary}
For $S\subsetneq T$ the map $w\mapsto\Vtrue(w)$ is strictly minimised at $w=0$: discarding the prefix is optimal, not merely acceptable.
\end{corollary}

\subsection{The audited geometry and the retraction}

\begin{theorem}[Quarter-prefix inflation]\label{thm:75}
If $S\subseteq T$ and $|T|=4|S|$ then $\Vtrue(\wivw) = \tfrac75\,\Vrep(\wivw)$.
\end{theorem}

This is exactly the audited configuration: $150\,000$ samples per modulus nested as a deterministic prefix inside $600\,000$. Write $z(d,v):=d/\sqrt v$.

\begin{lemma}
$z(d,fv) = z(d,v)/\sqrt f$ for $f\ge0$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{theorem}[Gate retraction]\label{thm:gate}
Let $d\ge0$, $v>0$ and suppose the reported statistic satisfies $z(d,v)\le 2.14$. Then $z\bigl(d,\tfrac75 v\bigr) < 1.96$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$z(d,\tfrac75v)=z(d,v)/\sqrt{7/5} \le 2.14/\sqrt{1.4}$, and $\sqrt{1.4}>1.1832$, so the bound is at most $1.809<1.96$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
The honest half-width of a nested pool is $\sqrt{7/5}\in(1.1832,\,1.1833)$ times the reported one; the reported interval spans about $84.5\%$ of the honest length. Hence the corrected interval $[0.9226,0.9966]$, which nominally excluded the null value $1$ at $z\approx2.14$, does not exclude it.
\end{corollary}

\section{Lineage and distinct-draw bounds}

\subsection{Chains}

\begin{lemma}[Chain covariance floor]\label{lem:chain}
Let $S_0\subseteq S_1\subseteq\cdots$ be nonempty legs of one design. For $i,j\le n$, $\Cov(\bar x_{S_i},\bar x_{S_j}) \ge \sigma^2/|S_n|$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{theorem}[Lineage bound]\label{thm:lineage}
For such a chain and weights $w_0,\dots,w_n\ge0$ with $\sum_i w_i=1$,
\[ \Var\Bigl(\sum_{i=0}^{n} w_i \bar x_{S_i}\Bigr) \ \ge\ \frac{\sigma^2}{|S_n|}. \]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Expand into $\sum_{i,j}w_iw_j\Cov(\bar x_{S_i},\bar x_{S_j})$, bound each covariance below by Lemma~\ref{lem:chain}, and use $(\sum_i w_i)^2=1$.
\end{proof}

A lineage of $n$ nested ``replications'', however weighted, carries exactly the information of its longest run.

\subsection{Arbitrary overlapping families}

Let $L$ be finite, $S_l$ nonempty legs, $w_l$ weights summing to $1$, and $U:=\bigcup_{l\in L}S_l$.

\begin{lemma}[Pools are linear forms]\label{lem:linform}
With $c_i := \sum_{l\in L}\mathbb 1[i\in S_l]\,w_l/|S_l|$ we have $\sum_l w_l\bar x_{S_l} = \sum_{i\in U}c_i x_i$ and $\sum_{i\in U}c_i = \sum_{l}w_l = 1$. Moreover $\Var\bigl(\sum_{i\in U}c_ix_i\bigr) = \sigma^2\sum_{i\in U}c_i^2$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{theorem}[Distinct-draw bound]\label{thm:distinct}
For any finite family of nonempty legs from one design and any weights summing to $1$,
\[ \Var\Bigl(\sum_{l\in L} w_l\bar x_{S_l}\Bigr) \ \ge\ \frac{\sigma^2}{|U|},\qquad U=\bigcup_{l\in L}S_l. \]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
By Lemma~\ref{lem:linform} the variance is $\sigma^2\sum_{i\in U}c_i^2$, and Cauchy--Schwarz gives $1=(\sum_{i\in U}c_i)^2 \le |U|\sum_{i\in U}c_i^2$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Sharpness]
The uniform pool $|U|^{-1}\sum_{i\in U}x_i$ attains the bound, with variance exactly $\sigma^2/|U|$.
\end{theorem}

The bound depends on nothing but the number of distinct draws consumed: not on the number of legs, not on the weights, not on how the analysis slices the stream. This is the exact sense in which \emph{one seed is one dataset}.

\subsection{What a fresh stream buys}

\begin{theorem}
If $S\cap T=\varnothing$ then $\Vtrue(\wivw)=\sigma^2/(|S|+|T|)$ (the reported value is honest), and $\Vtrue(\wivw) < \sigma^2/|T|$ --- in contrast with Theorem~\ref{thm:worse}. If additionally $|S|=|T|$ then $\Vtrue(\wivw) = \tfrac12\sigma^2/|T|$: error bars are divided by exactly $\sqrt2$.
\end{theorem}

The $\sqrt2$ here is the genuine gain that duplicate counting (Theorem~\ref{thm:dup}) manufactures fraudulently: the same factor, earned in one case and fabricated in the other.

\section{Shared populations: the overlap identity one level up}

\begin{definition}[Population design]
Fix $\sigma>0$ and $\rho\in[0,1]$. A \emph{population design} consists of vectors $u_i$ (shared component of object $i$) and $p_{i,t}$ (private component of draw $t$ on object $i$), mutually orthogonal in all distinct arguments, with $\inner{u_i}{u_i}=\rho\sigma^2$ and $\inner{p_{i,t}}{p_{i,t}}=(1-\rho)\sigma^2$. A readout is $o_{i,t}:=u_i+p_{i,t}$, of variance $\sigma^2$. For finite nonempty $K$ (objects) and $T$ (draw indices), the leg is $\bar o_{K,T} := (|K||T|)^{-1}\sum_{i\in K}\sum_{t\in T} o_{i,t}$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Population overlap identity]\label{thm:pop}
\[ \Cov\bigl(\bar o_{K,T},\bar o_{K',T'}\bigr) = \frac{\rho\sigma^2|K\cap K'|}{|K||K'|} + \frac{(1-\rho)\sigma^2|K\cap K'||T\cap T'|}{|K||T||K'||T'|}. \]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Split each leg sum as $|T|\sum_{i\in K}u_i + \sum_{(i,t)\in K\times T}p_{i,t}$; cross terms vanish by orthogonality. Lemma~\ref{lem:core} on the $u$'s gives $\rho\sigma^2|K\cap K'||T||T'|$, and on the $p$'s over product sets gives $(1-\rho)\sigma^2|K\cap K'||T\cap T'|$, using $(K\times T)\cap(K'\times T')=(K\cap K')\times(T\cap T')$. Divide by $|K||T||K'||T'|$.
\end{proof}

The two summands are exactly the two failure modes of the audit: shared draws (second term) and shared objects (first term).

\begin{corollary}[Two-level design effect]
$\Var(\bar o_{K,T}) = \rho\sigma^2/|K| + (1-\rho)\sigma^2/(|K||T|)$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{corollary}[Disjoint draws are not enough]\label{cor:notenough}
If $T\cap T'=\varnothing$ then $\Cov = \rho\sigma^2|K\cap K'|/(|K||K'|)$, strictly positive whenever $\rho>0$ and the populations meet. If $K\subseteq K'$ this is $\rho\sigma^2/|K'|>0$. Conversely, if $K\cap K'=\varnothing$ then $\Cov=0$ regardless of draw overlap.
\end{corollary}

\begin{theorem}[Quantified consequence]
For $K\subseteq K'$, disjoint draws, $\rho>0$ and $0<w<1$, the honest variance of the pooled estimator exceeds the reported inverse-variance value by exactly $2w(1-w)\rho\sigma^2/|K'| > 0$.
\end{theorem}

This is the precise sense in which the ``nominally independent'' two-leg combination fails: its legs are disjoint as \emph{measurement machinery} but nested as \emph{populations}, and only the latter controls the leading term. Since the population is generated deterministically from the master seed, re-drawing it within a fixed stream reproduces the same objects and the same covariance.

\section{The cluster ceiling}

\begin{definition}[Cluster model]
Fix $k$ clusters, $m$ draws per cluster, $\sigma>0$, $\rho\in[0,1]$, and centred readouts $y_{i,j}$ with $\inner{y_{i,j}}{y_{i,j}}=\sigma^2$, $\inner{y_{i,j}}{y_{i,j'}}=\rho\sigma^2$ for $j\ne j'$, and $\inner{y_{i,j}}{y_{i',j'}}=0$ for $i\ne i'$. The grand mean is $\bar y := (km)^{-1}\sum_{i,j}y_{i,j}$.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}
For every $(i,j)$: $\inner{y_{i,j}}{\sum_{i',j'}y_{i',j'}} = \sigma^2\bigl(1+(m-1)\rho\bigr)$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{theorem}[Design-effect identity]\label{thm:deff}
For $k,m\ge1$: $\Var(\bar y) = \sigma^2\bigl(1+(m-1)\rho\bigr)/(km)$.
\end{theorem}

Write $\Deff := 1+(m-1)\rho$ and $\neff := km/\Deff$.

\begin{theorem}[Clustering never helps]
$\Deff\ge1$ for $m\ge1$, with equality iff $\rho=0$ or $m=1$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Cluster ceiling]\label{thm:ceiling}
For $k,m\ge1$: $\Var(\bar y) \ge \rho\sigma^2/k$, uniformly in $m$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Cross-multiplying, the claim is $\rho m \le 1 + (m-1)\rho$, i.e.\ $\rho\le1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[The ceiling is attained]
For fixed $k\ge1$, $\displaystyle\lim_{m\to\infty}\frac{\sigma^2(1+(m-1)\rho)}{km} = \frac{\rho\sigma^2}{k}$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Information ceiling]\label{thm:ess}
If $\rho>0$ then $\neff \le k/\rho$ for every $m$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{corollary}[The audited run]\label{cor:12800}
With $k=128$ and $\rho=10^{-2}$, $\neff\le 12\,800$ for every $m$: the $76.8$-million-pair run carries no more information than $12\,800$ independent evaluations, a ratio of $6000{:}1$. At $m=600\,000$ the design effect is $1+599\,999/100 = 6000.99$, giving $\neff = 12\,798$ --- within $0.02\%$ of the ceiling.
\end{corollary}

Corollary~\ref{cor:12800} justifies cluster-level resampling for interval construction and rules out ``run it longer'' as a route to a decisive result.

\section{Optimal weights under known covariance}

For legs of variances $v_1,v_2$ and covariance $c$, set $f(w) := w^2v_1 + (1-w)^2v_2 + 2w(1-w)c$.

\begin{theorem}[Completed square]\label{thm:gls}
If $v_1+v_2-2c>0$ then for all $w$,
\[ f(w) = (v_1+v_2-2c)(w-w^\star)^2 + F,\qquad w^\star := \frac{v_2-c}{v_1+v_2-2c},\quad F := \frac{v_1v_2-c^2}{v_1+v_2-2c}. \]
Consequently $f(w)\ge F$ with equality iff $w=w^\star$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[When inverse-variance weighting is optimal]
Assuming $v_1+v_2>0$ and $v_1+v_2-2c>0$: $\dfrac{v_2}{v_1+v_2} = w^\star$ if and only if $c(v_2-v_1)=0$.
\end{theorem}

For legs of unequal precision, zero covariance is thus not merely convenient --- it is the exact condition under which the textbook weights are correct.

\begin{theorem}[Nested and disjoint specialisations]
For $S\subseteq T$ with $|S|<|T|$, substituting $v_1=\sigma^2/|S|$, $v_2=c=\sigma^2/|T|$ gives $w^\star=0$ and $F=\sigma^2/|T|$: discarding the prefix is the least-squares solution. For $S\cap T=\varnothing$, $c=0$ and $w^\star=|S|/(|S|+|T|)$: a genuinely fresh master seed restores the published procedure exactly.
\end{theorem}

\section{The audit, quantitatively}

The experiment measures a rate ratio $r$ between a candidate-side and a matched control-side hit rate for a smoothness property, at smoothness cuts $10^5$ (primary) and $10^6$ (secondary), against the null $r=1$. The run used $128$ moduli (band-9, $96$-bit semiprimes) and $600\,000$ paired samples each: $76.8$ million paired evaluations, $2.15\times$ the earlier pilot, at $68.1\,\mu\mathrm{s}$ per evaluation for a wall time of $5233.6$\,s.

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{lccc}
\toprule
Cut & $r$ & Events & Interval \\
\midrule
$10^5$ (primary)   & $0.9710$ & $2280/2348$   & $[0.8976,\ 1.0521]$ \\
$10^6$ (secondary) & $0.9623$ & $37255/38718$ & $[0.9224,\ 1.0040]$ \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

Both intervals (cluster bootstraps over the $128$ moduli) cover $1$; the run itself crosses no threshold.

\paragraph{Failure 1: the three-leg combination.} A joint over the pilot, a $150\,000$-per-modulus run and the $600\,000$-per-modulus run gave $r\approx0.971$ with interval $[0.942,1.000]$. The two later legs share a master seed end to end, and the longer run's draws are a strict superset of the shorter's: identical chunk seeds, deterministic prefix consumption, byte-identical first $150\,000$ samples per modulus including paired controls. By Theorem~\ref{thm:iff} the combination is invalid; by Theorem~\ref{thm:dup} it is arithmetically a self-pool reporting half the true variance; by Theorem~\ref{thm:lineage} no reweighting of the chain could have beaten the longest leg. It is withdrawn.

\paragraph{Failure 2: the two-leg fallback.} Combining only the pilot ($0.9468\pm0.0449$) and the long run ($0.9623\pm0.0208$) under independence gives $r=0.9596$ with $\sigma\approx0.0189$ and interval $[0.9226,0.9966]$ --- a $\approx4\%$ deficit excluding $1$ at $z\approx2.14$. But the pilot's $24$ moduli reconstruct inside the long run's $128$-modulus pool ($24/24$, zero rejects): the pilot's generator uses the same master-seed literal, an unconsumed-until-pools main generator, and a byte-identical prime-start primitive. By Corollary~\ref{cor:notenough} the covariance is $\rho\sigma^2/128>0$ whenever $\rho>0$, and by Theorem~\ref{thm:gate} a reported statistic of $2.14$ subject to the audited $7/5$ inflation has honest statistic below $1.96$. The exclusion is withdrawn.

\paragraph{Disposition.} Every dataset from the master seed in question is \emph{one} seed's evidence, jointly $3$--$5\%$ below $1$: a twice-gated candidate deviation, not a confirmation. The point estimate remains meaningful --- the measurement machinery of the two legs is genuinely disjoint, so the estimate is not double counted, only its precision was overstated --- but the edge does not survive. The candidate-side deficit is moreover \emph{opposite in sign} to the direction a known compensating mechanism (a quadratic-residue sieve advantage) would predict; if real it is a new weak effect at the relevant smoothness scale.

\paragraph{The decisive step.} By Corollary~\ref{cor:12800} more samples on the same $128$ moduli are worthless (the run is within $0.02\%$ of the cluster floor); by Theorem~\ref{thm:distinct} more legs from the same stream are worthless. Only a fresh master seed --- new population, new stream, disjoint at both levels --- moves the floor, and an equal-size disjoint replication delivers a genuine $\sqrt2$ tightening.

\section{The design rule, as a corollary}

The rule adopted after this audit --- \emph{replication legs must vary the master seed, and scripts must assert seed distinctness in their own output} --- follows from the theorems:
\begin{enumerate}
\item Independence requires disjointness of draws (Theorem~\ref{thm:iff}), which cannot be certified from summary statistics, only from the stream.
\item Draw-disjointness alone is insufficient when the population is shared (Corollary~\ref{cor:notenough}).
\item A deterministically generated population is a function of the master seed, so ``re-draw the population'' inside a fixed stream changes neither the objects nor the covariance. Varying the master seed is the only operation making both levels disjoint at once.
\item Nothing else buys precision: draws per object are capped by $\rho\sigma^2/k$ (Theorem~\ref{thm:ceiling}), legs from one stream by $\sigma^2/|U|$ (Theorem~\ref{thm:distinct}), and weights by the GLS floor (Theorem~\ref{thm:gls}), which for a nested pair is the long leg's own variance.
\end{enumerate}

\section{Algorithms}

\paragraph{Overlap-aware pooling.} Given legs $S_1,\dots,S_n$ as sets of stream positions and $\sigma^2$, compute $C_{lm}=\sigma^2|S_l\cap S_m|/(|S_l||S_m|)$ by Theorem~\ref{thm:master}; solve $\min_w w^\top Cw$ subject to $\mathbf 1^\top w = 1$ (closed form for $n=2$ by Theorem~\ref{thm:gls}); report $w^\top Cw$, never the diagonal-only value. Complexity $O(n^2\bar s)$ for intersections plus $O(n^3)$ for the solve.

\paragraph{Design-effect audit.} From per-cluster counts estimate $\rho$ via between- and within-cluster mean squares; report $\neff = km/(1+(m-1)\hat\rho)$ and the ceiling $k/\hat\rho$ (Theorem~\ref{thm:ess}); construct intervals by resampling clusters with replacement, never draws.

\paragraph{Stream-provenance check.} Given two run manifests (master seed, chunk-seed rule, per-modulus counts, population primitive), return \texttt{nested} (equal seeds and equal chunk rules; inflation $(3|S|+|T|)/(|S|+|T|)$), \texttt{population-shared} (equal seeds, differing consumption, intersecting populations; covariance $\rho\sigma^2|K\cap K'|/(|K||K'|)$), \texttt{disjoint} (distinct seeds with verified distinct populations), or \texttt{unknown}. Linear in manifest size plus population reconstruction.

\section{Discussion}

The design effect and the inverse-variance formula are classical. What is contributed here is exactness and an ``if and only if''. Overlap is usually treated as a source of ``some'' underestimation to be handled conservatively; Theorem~\ref{thm:defect} makes it a number computable from three cardinalities, Theorem~\ref{thm:iff} shows there is no intermediate regime between disjoint and defective, Theorem~\ref{thm:worse} refutes the folk principle that more data cannot hurt, and Theorem~\ref{thm:distinct} shows no analytic ingenuity extracts more than the distinct draws contain. Theorem~\ref{thm:pop} unifies shared draws and shared objects into one identity, which is what makes a mechanical provenance audit possible.

The model is a second-moment model: everything is exact for arbitrary joint distributions with the stated covariances, but nothing is claimed about higher moments or bias. Equicorrelation within a cluster is an idealisation; with heterogeneous correlations the ceiling becomes $\bar\rho\sigma^2/k$ for an appropriate average $\bar\rho$, unchanged qualitatively. The identification of ``run overlap'' with ``index-set intersection'' presumes a deterministic, reproducible consumption order --- exactly the setting in which the failure occurs.

We stress what the audit does \emph{not} establish. Nothing here says the observed $3$--$5\%$ deficit is spurious; the point estimate is unaffected by the variance defects, and only the claimed precision is. The correct summary is that the evidence for a deviation is one seed's worth, which is less than it appeared to be. The run's own intervals, being cluster bootstraps, are consistent with Theorem~\ref{thm:ceiling}; only the \emph{cross-run} combinations failed, which is why the intra-run verdict survives intact.

\section{Future directions}

\textbf{An intra-cluster correlation law.} The ceiling $k/\rho$ makes $\rho$ the most valuable unknown in the design. A predictive law for $\rho$ as a function of modulus size, smoothness cut and band would turn planning into optimisation: for a budget $B=km$, maximise $km/(1+(m-1)\rho)$, which for known $\rho$ favours more clusters until per-cluster fixed costs dominate. Estimating $\hat\rho$ from archived per-cluster counts is cheap and should be routine.

\textbf{Sequential designs with provenance constraints.} Theorem~\ref{thm:distinct} suggests that the marginal value of a new leg is governed by $|U\cup S_{\mathrm{new}}| - |U|$. Maximising new-draw yield per unit compute subject to population-disjointness is a well-posed combinatorial optimisation problem.

\textbf{Hierarchical correlations.} Extending Theorem~\ref{thm:pop} to three levels (band, modulus, draw) would allow auditing designs that share a band but not a modulus --- the next-finest failure mode.

\textbf{Sharp lineage bounds under partial overlap.} Theorem~\ref{thm:distinct} is tight for the uniform pool over $U$; for structured families (chains, prefix trees) one may ask for the tight bound as a function of the overlap poset.

\textbf{Automatic provenance certificates.} The provenance check should be a compulsory pre-registration artefact accompanying each reported combination, so that a defective pooling cannot reach publication.

\section{Conclusion}

Three cardinalities decide whether a pooled estimate is honest: the sizes of two index sets and the size of their intersection. From this follow an exact defect law, an iff-characterisation of valid inverse-variance pooling, a bound on what an entire lineage of runs from one seed can ever be worth, a two-level identity catching shared populations as well as shared draws, and a ceiling making long runs on a fixed set of objects futile beyond a computable point. Applied to a $76.8$-million-pair randomness experiment, these retract one combination outright, dissolve the apparent threshold crossing of another, and leave a coherent verdict: both intervals cover the null, the deficit is a twice-gated candidate, and the only informative next move is a genuinely fresh master seed.

